<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cristero War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1926">1926</a>–<a href="/years/1929">1929</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cristero War (1926–1929) was an armed uprising in central and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> against the anticlerical laws of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarco-elias-calles">Plutarco Elías Calles</a>. Catholic rebels, the Cristeros, fought federal troops chiefly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalisco">Jalisco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michoacan">Michoacán</a> and neighbouring states, and tens of thousands died. It ended with an agreement between the government and the Church brokered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> ambassador Dwight Morrow.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cristero-war.jpg" alt="Cristero War" width="320" height="359" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zbitka_sze%C5%9Bciu_ilustracji_z_Powstania_Cristero_1926-1929.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
